)[
  *Summary.* A geometric algebra is a vector space whose elements can be
  multiplied, with the product encoding both the metric and the exterior
  structure. In robotics, the useful models are the Euclidean algebra
  $"Cl"(3,0,0)$ (rotations), the projective algebra $"Cl"(3,0,1)$ (incidence,
  lines and planes, dual quaternions as its even part), and the conformal
  algebra $"Cl"(4,1)$ (points, spheres, circles, planes, lines, and the full
  Möbius group in one algebraic system). The conformal model adds two
  dimensions to $RR^3$ and represents a Euclidean point by a *null vector*.
  Distances, angles, incidence and intersections then become inner and outer
  products, and rigid motions become *motors*, unit even elements that act by
  sandwiching. This note fixes one set of conventions, derives the formulas
  the library needs, and points out where the representation choices become
  software decisions.
]

#v(0.4em)

= The algebra in one page

== Quadratic spaces and Clifford algebras

Let $V$ be a real vector space with a symmetric bilinear form $g$, and write
$v^2 = g(v,v)$. The *Clifford algebra* $"Cl"(V,g)$ is the associative algebra
generated by $V$ subject to

$ u v + v u = 2 g(u,v), quad u,v in V. $

Choose an orthonormal basis $e_1, dots, e_n$ with $e_i^2 = sigma_i$, where
$sigma_i in {+1,-1,0}$. A *signature* $(p,q,r)$ means $p$ positive, $q$
negative and $r$ null basis directions. The products

$ e_A = e_(i_1) e_(i_2) dots e_(i_k), quad A = {i_1 < dots < i_k} subset.eq {1,dots,n}, $

together with $e_(emptyset) = 1$, form a basis of $"Cl"(V,g)$, so the algebra
has dimension $2^n$. The integer $k$ is the *grade* of the blade $e_A$; a
general element is a sum of terms of different grades, called a *multivector*.
With an orthonormal basis the product of two basis blades is again a basis
blade up to a sign:

$ e_A e_B = epsilon(A,B) e_(A xor B), quad epsilon(A,B) in {-1,+1}, $

where $A xor B$ is the symmetric difference of the index sets and the sign
$epsilon(A,B)$ is the parity of the permutation that sorts the concatenation
of $A$ and $B$ (and, if some $sigma_i = 0$, is zero whenever $i in A inter B$).
This one rule is all the algebra there is; the rest of this note unpacks it.

