Three remarks matter for the software design.

- The incidence test is uniform: for any object blade $O$ in the working
  representation and any point $X$, $X$ lies on the object exactly when
  $X dot O = 0$. For a point object $O = X_1$ this is the statement that the
  distance is zero; for a point pair it selects the two points.
- The dual of a point is a 4-blade (the pencil of planes through the point),
  not another point. The dual operation is an involution up to the sign
  $I^2 = -1$; `undual` must be defined explicitly, not as `dual` twice.
- A generic `meet` is only uniform for grade-one constraints. For two planes
  or spheres, the intersection is the outer product and the result is in the
  working representation: two planes give a line, two spheres give a circle,
  a plane and a sphere give a circle. When higher-grade constraints are
  involved, the outer product can be a dual representation: the wedge of a
  line and a sphere is the grade-three dual of the point pair, and one
  dualises it to get the pair in the working representation; incident
  constraints can wedge to zero. Points are direct objects, and mixing a point
  with a constraint in the same `meet` is not the wedge. The library exposes
  the outer product and the named constructors rather than pretending one
  operation does everything.

= Transformations

== Versors and the conformal group

A versor $V$ acts on every object by $O mapsto V O V^(-1)$. Because this is an
algebra automorphism, grades and all incidence relations are preserved. In
$"Cl"(4,1)$ the versor group is the spin group $"Spin"(4,1)$, a double cover
of $"SO"(4,1)$; the latter is the Möbius group of $RR^3$, of dimension ten. Its
ten generators, as bivectors, are

- three rotations: $e_23, e_31, e_12$;
- three translations: $e_1 and e_oo, e_2 and e_oo, e_3 and e_oo$;
- one dilation: $e_4 e_5$;
- three transversions (special conformal transformations):
  $e_1 and e_0, e_2 and e_0, e_3 and e_0$.

The first six preserve $e_oo$ and form the Lie algebra of $"SE"(3)$; the
dilation scales $e_oo$ and $e_0$; the transversions do not preserve $e_oo$ and
complete the Möbius algebra. For robotics we use the first six all the time,
the dilation occasionally (for scale-normalised objectives), and the
transversions rarely; they are nevertheless part of the model and cheap to
provide once the general machinery exists.

== Rotors

For a unit Euclidean bivector $B$ (a rotation plane) and an angle $theta$,

$ R = exp(-theta/2 B) = cos(theta/2) - sin(theta/2) B. $

The sandwich $X mapsto R X tilde(R)$ rotates $bold(x)$ by $theta$ about the
origin and leaves $e_0$ and $e_oo$ alone. A rotation about an axis through the
point $C$ is the conjugate $T_C R T_C^(-1)$, where $T_C$ is the translator of
§5.3. Rotors form the even subalgebra of the Euclidean part of the model; in
three dimensions this is the quaternion algebra.

== Translators

A translation by $bold(t)$ is generated by the bivector $-1/2 bold(t) e_oo$.
Because $(bold(t) e_oo)^2 = norm(bold(t))^2 e_oo^2 = 0$, the series terminates:

$ T = exp(-1/2 bold(t) e_oo) = 1 - 1/2 bold(t) e_oo. $

Its action is $X mapsto T X tilde(T)$; expanding the three terms in $X$ gives
the point of $bold(x) + bold(t)$ (the correction to the $e_oo$ coefficient is
exactly the $1/2 norm(bold(t))^2$ needed to keep the image null). A translator
is even, unit, and fixes $e_oo$.

== Motors

A rigid motion is a rotation followed by a translation:

$ M = T R, quad M tilde(M) = 1, quad X mapsto M X tilde(M). $

$M$ is an even element of the subalgebra generated by $e_(i j)$ and
$e_i e_oo$. That subalgebra is eight-dimensional, with basis

$ {1, e_12, e_13, e_23, e_1 e_oo, e_2 e_oo, e_3 e_oo, e_123 e_oo}, $

and it is isomorphic to the *dual quaternions* (the even subalgebra of
$"Cl"(3,0,1)$). This is the single most important implementation fact for
robotics: a motor does not need 32 coefficients, only 8. The full even
subalgebra of $"Cl"(4,1)$ is 16-dimensional, but the rigid-motion part is the
8-dimensional subalgebra above. The library therefore has two types: a dense
multivector `t` for objects and general algebra, and a packed `Motor.t` with
eight components for kinematics. Rotation-only and translation-only motors are
the special cases built from a rotation bivector and a translation vector above.

== Dilators and transversions

A dilation by the factor $e^lambda$ is generated by $e_4 e_5$:

$ D = exp(-lambda/2 e_4 e_5) = cosh(lambda/2) - sinh(lambda/2) e_4 e_5. $

The sandwich $D X tilde(D)$ is not normalised on the point slice: the image is
a null vector whose $e_0$ coefficient is $e^(-lambda)$, so one divides by that
coefficient (equivalently, by $-e_oo dot X'$) to read the transformed point.
After normalisation the map is $bold(x) mapsto e^lambda bold(x)$. This is a
useful reminder that a versor action on the null cone is projective: the
representative is only defined up to scale, and the point slice fixes the
scale.

A transversion with parameter vector $bold(a)$ is generated by
$-bold(a) dot (e_i and e_0)$. The bivector is nilpotent, so

$ K = exp(-a_i e_i and e_0) = 1 - a_i e_i e_0, $

and the normalised action is the special conformal transformation

$ bold(x) mapsto (bold(x) + bold(a) norm(bold(x))^2) slash (1 + 2 bold(a) dot bold(x) + norm(bold(a))^2 norm(bold(x))^2). $

Again the raw image is off the point slice and must be renormalised. The
formula is a useful test case: it is nonlinear on $RR^3$ but linear on the
conformal null cone, which is the whole point of the embedding.

== Lie algebra, twists, and the SE(3) exponential

The bivectors of $"Cl"(4,1)$ form the Lie algebra $"so"(4,1)$ under
the commutator product. The rigid-motion subalgebra is spanned by the three
rotation generators and the three translation generators; a general element is
a *twist*

$ xi = (omega, bold(v)) in RR^6, $

with $omega$ the angular velocity and $bold(v)$ the linear velocity. The
corresponding motor is the $"SE"(3)$ exponential. In matrix form, with
$hat(omega)$ the skew matrix of $omega$, $theta = norm(omega)$ and

$ V = "Id" + (1 - cos theta)/theta^2 hat(omega) + (theta - sin theta)/theta^3 hat(omega)^2, $

one has $R = exp(theta hat(omega))$, $bold(t) = V bold(v)$, and
$M = (R, bold(t))$. The logarithm is the inverse:

$ theta = arccos((op("tr") R - 1)/2), quad omega = theta/(2 sin theta) (R - R^T), quad bold(v) = V^(-1) bold(t), $

where the skew matrix $R - R^T$ is identified with its axis vector in $RR^3$.

The library implements this on the packed motor type; the dense bivector
`exp` is the same map expressed in the 32-dimensional algebra, and tests check
that the two agree. The general bivector exponential (including dilations and
transversions) is a later, separate routine.

== Interpolation and the adjoint

Because motors form a Lie group, interpolation is the group exponential:

$ M(lambda) = M_0 exp(lambda log(M_0^(-1) M_1)), quad lambda in [0,1], $

the *screw linear interpolation* (ScLERP) of rigid motions. It is the
motor analogue of quaternion slerp and is the reason to keep `exp` and `log`
in the public interface, not just in the kinematics layer.

For a fixed motor $M$, the sandwich $X mapsto M X tilde(M)$ is linear in $X$;
its matrix in the blade basis is the *adjoint* $op("Ad")_M$. For the dense
algebra it is a $32 times 32$ matrix; for the packed motor it is the usual
$6 times 6$ adjoint of $"SE"(3)$ acting on twists (and its dual action on
wrenches). The dense adjoint is useful for batching: transforming a large
batch of points by one motor becomes a matrix multiply, and it is a convenient
test of the sandwich implementation.

= Using it in robotics

== Forward kinematics

A kinematic chain is a product of motors. For each joint $i$ with configuration
$q_i$, let $M_i(q_i)$ be the motor of the joint (a rotor about an axis through
a point for a revolute joint, a translator for a prismatic joint, the
exponential of a twist for a helical joint), and let $F_i$ be the fixed
transform from the previous link frame to the joint. Then

$ M(q) = F_1 M_1(q_1) F_2 M_2(q_2) dots F_n M_n(q_n) F_(n+1), $

and the pose of any point or object attached to the tool is obtained by one
sandwich. Every operation here is an 8-component motor product followed by a
32-component sandwich; both are small, batched, and differentiable.

== Constraints and objectives

The conformal model turns geometric predicates into algebra. A point $X$ on a
sphere $S$, a plane $pi$, a line $ell$ or a circle $z$ is the single scalar
equation $X dot O = 0$. A distance is $-2 X dot Y$ under a square root. A
point-to-plane signed distance is $X dot pi$. An orientation constraint can be
written with the bivector part of a motor, and a sphere-sphere or
plane-sphere intersection is an outer product. These expressions are
polynomial in the coefficients (apart from the final square roots), which
makes them good residuals for optimisation and good targets for automatic
differentiation.

== Inverse kinematics

The standard local IK step uses the error twist
$xi = log(M_"target" M_"current"^(-1))$ and the geometric Jacobian, whose
columns are the joint twists expressed in the tool frame. The update is a
Gauss-Newton or Levenberg-Marquardt step on $xi$. With motors, the error is
computed by `log`, the Jacobian columns are either analytic twists or obtained
by differentiating the chain with `Rune`, and the update is applied by `exp`.
The same loop works for task-space objectives written as conformal residuals:
stack the scalar residuals, differentiate, and solve.

== Why the conformal model, and when not to use it

CGA gives one algebra for points, spheres, planes, lines, circles, rigid
motions, dilations and inversions, with uniform incidence and distance
formulas. That is valuable when a task mixes object geometry (grasping a
cylinder, keeping a point on a plane, intersecting a line with a sphere) with
kinematics. It is not the cheapest representation for pure rigid-motion
algebra: the packed motor type is exactly dual quaternions, and if a task only
ever needs poses and twists, dual quaternions or $"SE"(3)$ matrices are leaner.
The design keeps the motor layer independent of the conformal object layer so
that both uses are available without paying for the other.

= Lower signatures: projective and Euclidean

== Euclidean 3D

$"Cl"(3,0,0)$ is the rotation algebra. Its even subalgebra is the quaternions;
its bivectors are the rotation planes; its vectors are Euclidean vectors. It
is the right model for orientation-only problems and the simplest testbed for
the generic algebra implementation. The conformal model contains it: the
rotors of §5.2 are even elements of the Euclidean subalgebra generated by
$e_1,e_2,e_3$.

== Projective 3D: the degenerate signature

$"Cl"(3,0,1)$ adds one *degenerate* basis vector $e_0$ with $e_0^2 = 0$. The
objects are:

- a plane is a vector $pi = bold(n) + d e_0$;
- a line is a bivector, the wedge of two planes;
- a point is a trivector, the wedge of three planes (equivalently, the dual of
  a plane);
- a point $p$ lies on a plane $pi$ exactly when $p and pi = 0$;
- ideal points (directions) are the trivectors of zero weight, with vanishing
  coefficient on $e_123$.

The pseudoscalar has $I^2 = 0$, so there is no inverse and no Hodge dual. The
dual is the *complement* of §1.6, and the regressive product is defined by

$ A or B = (A^c and B^c)^c, $

where $A^c$ is the complement of $A$ (with the precomputed signs). Incidence is
the wedge $p and pi = 0$; because the metric is degenerate, it does *not*
reduce to a scalar contraction of the dual point with the plane, as it does in
the conformal model. This is a real difference between the two object layers,
not a convention. In this model the meet of two planes is their outer product
(a line), the join of two points is the regressive product (also a line), and
the whole incidence calculus is exact and polynomial. This is the algebra of choice for
plane-based projective geometry and for graphics; it is also the even
subalgebra that gives dual quaternions.

== The shared motor algebra

The subalgebra of $"Cl"(4,1)$ generated by $e_1,e_2,e_3,e_oo$ is isomorphic to
$"Cl"(3,0,1)$: the four generators have squares $1,1,1,0$ and anticommute. Its
even part is the eight-dimensional motor algebra of §5.4, with $e_oo$ playing
the role of the degenerate $e_0$. This is why the packed motor type can be
shared between the conformal and projective models: the product of two motors,
the inverse, the exponential and the logarithm are the same operations in
both. What is *not* shared is the object layer: a CGA point is a null vector,
a PGA point is a trivector, and the two embeddings of the motor into the
32-dimensional or 16-dimensional algebra are different. The library therefore
puts `Motor` at the top level and gives each model its own `of_motor` /
`apply` functions.

