= Computing with the algebra

== Blades as bitmasks

Number the basis vectors from $1$ to $n$ and encode a blade $e_A$ by the
integer whose bit $i-1$ is set exactly when $i in A$. Then

- the grade is the population count of the integer;
- the product index is the bitwise exclusive or, $A xor B$;
- the sign is a function of the two index sets only;
- the metric factor is the product of $sigma_i$ over the intersection
  $A inter B$, and is zero if a null direction appears in both factors.

For an orthonormal basis this gives the whole multiplication table over
$O(2^(2n))$ blade pairs, with no floating-point work. For $n = 5$ there are
$32$ blades and $1024$ ordered pairs; the table is tiny. The coefficient
vector of a multivector is an array of length $2^n$, and the geometric product
is

$ (A B)_K = sum_(I xor J = K) epsilon(I,J) m(I,J) A_I B_J. $

This is a batched contraction over the last axis when $A$ and $B$ carry batch
dimensions. The implementation can use the explicit $2^n times 2^n times 2^n$
structure tensor in an `einsum`, or the equivalent gather-and-sum with the
xor table; both are traceable, and the choice is a performance question for
the benchmarks.

== Why the library stores an orthonormal basis

The null basis $(e_1,e_2,e_3,e_0,e_oo)$ is the right basis for *reading* the
geometry: points have simple coordinates, spheres and planes are vectors, and
the formulas of §2–§4 are short. But it is not orthogonal, and the bitmask
product rule assumes orthogonality: in a non-orthogonal basis the product of
two distinct basis vectors is not a single blade, but a blade plus a scalar
(or more). Conjugating the whole product table into the null basis is
possible, but it destroys the sparse xor structure and complicates every
grade-level optimisation. The library therefore stores coefficients in the
orthonormal frame $(e_1,dots,e_5)$ and defines the named elements $e_0$ and
$e_oo$ as the linear combinations above. Points and spheres then have a
handful of nonzero coefficients in the orthonormal frame, just as they do in
the null basis; the difference is that the product rule stays sparse and the
grade structure stays uniform. In PGA the natural basis
is already orthogonal ($e_1,e_2,e_3,e_0$ with $e_0^2=0$), so no such choice is
needed there.

== Batching and shapes

Every value is an `Nx` array whose *last* axis is the blade axis. A single
multivector has shape $[2^n]$; a batch of $B$ points has shape $[B, 2^n]$; an
image of points has shape $[H, W, 2^n]$. The leading axes are ordinary
broadcasting batch axes, so a constructor such as `point` takes coordinate
tensors and returns a batch of conformal points, and an extractor
returns a coordinate tensor without the blade axis. The packed motor type is
the same convention with last axis $8$. All operations are pure `Nx`; there is
no host-side branching on tensor values, which keeps the whole algebra
compatible with `Rune.jit` and `Rune.grad`.

== Exponentials in a traced setting

The closed forms of §1.7 and §5.6 are the primary implementation. They are
built from `Nx` primitives (`sin`, `cos`, `sinh`, `cosh`, `sqrt`, `atan2`) and
contain no data-dependent OCaml branching: small-angle limits are handled by
`sinc`-style rewrites, and any unavoidable choice is made with `Nx.where` and
safe denominators. For the general bivector exponential, a
scaling-and-squaring scheme with
a fixed number of squarings and a truncated series is the traceable fallback;
its accuracy and cost will be characterised in the tests before it is exposed.

== Numerical cautions

The conformal model is projectively normalised, so every object has a scale
that must be fixed before comparisons: points by $e_oo dot X = -1$, spheres by
$e_oo dot S = -1$, planes by $pi^2 = 1$. Motor exponentials and logarithms
need the usual small-angle safeguards (`atan2`, `sinc`-like limits) and a
normalisation step. Imaginary spheres have $S^2 < 0$, while a point pair of
two real points has $P^2 > 0$ (its reverse norm is negative); neither sign
should be forced positive before a square root. The library returns signed
squared magnitudes and takes absolute values only where the mathematics calls
for them.
Finally, the Hodge dual is only defined for non-degenerate signatures; the
degenerate branches use the complement, and the two must not be mixed in a
generic `dual` that silently does the wrong thing.

